A note is evidence, not requirements. It describes what the reviewer saw in
{{ .Finding.From }}'s diff; most of each one is about {{ .Finding.From }}, and only the
part naming this finding is this item's.
{{ else }}This scaffold was filed while reviewing {{ .Finding.From }}, and the ledger records no audit notes against {{ .Finding.From }}.
{{ end }}{{ end }}
The numbered list is the contract. `drover report --save` turns every
top-level line that opens with a number, a period and a space into an audit
checkbox. A requirement written as prose is on no checklist and nobody will
ever confirm it, so the whole list has to be numbered and each line has to be
one falsifiable claim.

Read the code before you write a line of it. An item saying "improve the
handler" produces a run that improves something; one citing
`path/to/file.go:120-145` produces a run that can be checked. Cite the files
and the lines you actually read.
