{{- if .StopMarker }}
Leave the stop marker in place:

    {{ .StopMarker }}

Deleting it is the act of accepting these requirements, and that is the
operator's, not yours. Drafting and accepting are different acts: you propose,
a human reads what you proposed and takes the marker out. Until they do,
`drover run {{ .ID }}` refuses this item and so does `--dry-run`, which is the
guarantee that nothing you wrote executes unread.
{{ end }}
{{- block "context" . }}{{ end }}
Then:
- Say what you read to arrive at each requirement, and what you could not
  establish. A requirement you could not ground in the code is a question for
  the operator, not a line in the list.
- Do not implement any of it. The body is the deliverable.
- Do not delete the stop marker, and do not add a flag, a comment or a note
  asking somebody else to delete it on your behalf.
